the representation of women on company  boards is on the agenda as EU ministers  responsible for employment Social Policy  health and consumer affairs meet in  Luxembourg discussion will be based on a  progress report on possible measures to  improve gender balance at the top of  Europe's companies the European  Commission wants to increase the number  of women on non executive boards to  forty percent by 2020 EU nations support  the principle of improving the gender  balance although some countries believe  this should be done at the national  level while others prefer eu-wide  legislation health ministers will be  updated on draft rules for medical  devices like corrective glasses dental  filling material x-ray machines heart  valves or breast implants and on in  vitro diagnostic devices like blood  tests the revision of EU laws on medical  devices is aimed at providing high  protection for European patients  consumers and healthcare professionals  and to ensure that safe and effective  equipment is readily available ministers  will also look at what impact the  economic crisis had on health care  systems and suggest ways to make the  sector more resilient to future shocks  the council will also look at nutrition  and physical activity given that up to  seven percent of EU health budgets are  directly spent each year on disease  linked to obesity the minister's aim to  propose measures to encourage the  promotion of physical activity and a  healthy diet
 

